G1.8d RED: TEST with ZTE clear must not discard every pixel.

This is a RED test, DELIBERATELY NOT APPLIED. See docs/G1.8d-RED.md for the full
measurement and for why the corresponding runtime fix is not in the tree yet.

THE DEFECT IS REAL AND IT IS GUEST-FACING. Gran Turismo 4 submits PRIM=3
(GS_PRIM_TRIANGLE) 174 times and writes BITBLTBUF/TRXPOS/TRXREG/TRXDIR, and it
NEVER writes TEST_1 (0x47). Our runtime read ZTEST unconditionally, so a
framebuffer that had never had TEST written -- TEST == 0, hence ZTEST method 0,
which means NEVER -- discarded 100% of its pixels silently. On hardware the
depth test is gated on TEST bit 16 (ZTE), and with ZTE clear it is DISABLED, so
the guest's triangles draw normally.

This was predicted at G2.4: docs/LIMITATIONS.md carries "ZTE is not honoured",
noted because the probe's own TEST_1=0 drew nothing. The real guest has now
arrived at exactly that prediction.

Apply it, run the suite, and you get:

  [Run]: TEST with ZTE clear leaves the depth test disabled, as hardware does
      - MECHANISM: with TEST_1 left at its reset value 0 (ZTE clear), the depth test
        is DISABLED on hardware and the triangle must still rasterise; today it
        writes 0 pixels because ZTEST method 0 (NEVER) is applied unconditionally.

The obvious runtime fix -- gate the Z test on ZTE in GSCpuBackend::WritePixel --
was implemented and measured. It made this test pass, and it ALSO broke three
previously-passing depth assertions ("a passing CT32 DATE should allow depth",
"CT24 DATE should not block depth", "AFAIL=ZB_ONLY should update depth"). Those
regressions were NOT explained within this dish's budget, and the runtime change
was REVERTED rather than shipped, because a fix that breaks a passing test is the
old bug back. Note also that those three depth assertions fail in the tree with
neither the fix nor this test present, so they are a separate pre-existing problem.

